      "The Supervisory System",
      "FINRA and the SEC can act against any firm that fails to supervise its reps. But supervisors may not be held responsible if a good system is in place to prevent and detect violations. Each firm must have written supervisory procedures, with regular inspections and reviews.\n\nA supervisory system must:\n• Name its Offices of Supervisory Jurisdiction (OSJs) and how they are managed.\n• Set a chain of command.\n• Create and keep written supervisory procedures.\n• Hold an annual compliance meeting with every principal and rep. Prerecorded webcasts are fine if people can ask questions and get timely answers.\n\nAn office must be an OSJ if it does any of these: executes orders or makes markets, structures offerings or private placements, holds customer funds or securities, gives final approval of new accounts, reviews and endorses customer orders, gives final approval of retail communications, or supervises reps at branch offices. Firms may also make an office an OSJ if it has regular customer contact, is far from another OSJ, or has many reps doing complex work.\n\nEach OSJ needs at least one general principal. That person enforces the procedures, reviews accounts, keeps customer records and complaints, and inspects branch offices.",
