      "Branches, Inspections, and Home Offices",
      "A branch office is any place where reps ask customers to buy or sell securities but do not execute trades. An office that supervises non-branch locations is also a branch.\n\nInspections:\n• Every OSJ and every branch that supervises non-branch locations: every year.\n• Other branch offices: every 3 years.\nInspections review customer account records for problems and test the rules for books, records, and handling customer money. Each inspection needs a written report kept for 3 years.\n\nA rep working from home is not usually a branch if no customers are met there and no checks or securities are taken there. Reps must send all email through official firm channels, not personal email.\n\nNon-branch locations include:\n• Offices used only for customer service or back-office work, with no sales.\n• Places used fewer than 30 business days a year for securities business.\n• The floor of a registered exchange.\n• A home where only family members who live there work, no customers are met, no money or securities are handled, it is assigned to a branch, and all orders go through that branch.\n\nWritten procedures must list all supervisors' names, titles, registrations, locations, and duties, and explain how trades and correspondence are reviewed. A copy of the procedures and the FINRA Manual must be kept at each OSJ and supervisory location.",
